  "salt": "-d6MePlPMJ7Du-wCg5Ss6g==",
  "text": "Manannán mac Lir was an Irish god of the ocean. He was the son of Lir, an older and obscure god of the sea. Manannán never lived on land. He made his home on an island called Mag Mell, Tír Tairngiri, and Emain Ablach, the last connected at times to the Arthurian otherworld of Avalon. His name means the little one of Man or the son of the sea. Both Gaels and Britons called him filius maris, the son of the sea. Manannán was a master of shape-shifting, like the Greek Proteus. He could give the same power to those he cherished. His magic boat of copper sailed faster than the wind, drawn by a horse named Enbharr, whose hair was the foam of the waves. He could make a dozen men look like an army and a handful of chips look like an armada. His magic cloak caused forgetfulness when he shook it out. By the tenth century he was absorbed into the Tuatha Dé Danann. He gave the tribe of Danu three gifts: the druid's fog, the feast of Goibniu, and his magical pigs. The pigs were killed and eaten each day and came alive again each morning. The Isle of Man is named for him, and he was its first king. Koch records that the Isle of Man is his home and sometimes his personification. It stands central to the mythical geography of medieval Gaelic literature.",
